Tag every pointer event with its device and whether it was synthesized from touch.

What: `TouchEvent` gets a `device: PointerDevice` field (Mouse, Touch, Pen), and `BackGestureEvent` a `source:
BackGestureSource` (`EdgeSwipe` or `Key`). The new `Window::pointer_source()` returns a `PointerSource { device,
synthesized }` for the pointer event being dispatched, and `None` otherwise (key events, render, after dispatch
returns). Render sees `None` even for the draw a tap runs before its dispatch returns. `dispatch_event` sets it from a
table with one arm per variant (`pointer_source`):
- backend mouse, scroll, pinch and file-drop input is `Mouse`, not synthesized;
- a raw touch is its own device, not synthesized;
- an edge-swipe back gesture, and the platform's long press and touch drag (which name no touch), are `Touch`,
  synthesized; a key-driven back gesture (Android's Back key) is `None`, like a key.
Every tap, pan and long press that the core recognizer builds from a touch carries that touch's device, synthesized:
the recognizer's per-touch state (`ActiveTouch`, and `Momentum` for the fling it leaves) records the device, so a pen
and a finger down together each label their own gestures. The value is saved and restored around each dispatch, so
neither a nested dispatch nor a timer-driven gesture (long press, momentum) can leave it stale. iOS reads
`UITouch.type`: Pencil is `Pen`, an iPad pointer's indirect touch is `Mouse`; its edge swipe is `EdgeSwipe`. Web
touches are `Touch`.

Why: GPUI turns taps into mouse presses and pans into scroll events, so a listener cannot tell a finger from a mouse.
The only signal is the window-wide `last_input_was_touch()`. Platform behaviour (touch targets, selection handles,
hover) depends on the device behind the event in hand. A field on every mouse and scroll event would touch ~95 struct
literals across all backends; a per-dispatch query carries the same information without changing those types. A
back gesture can come from a key with no pointer behind it, so it says which.

Known limit: web pens take the mouse path (`pointerType == "pen"` is not a touch), so they read as `Mouse`. Only a
device field on mouse events could carry that.

Test: `pointer_source_names_the_device_and_synthesis_of_each_event` (window.rs), one row per input kind (pen and
finger down together, a finger after a pen, edge-swipe and key back included); each row also records what render
sees.

Size: the diff is ~800 lines but adds ~375, and ~205 of those are the test. It is not split: the new
`TouchEvent::device` and `BackGestureEvent::source` fields break every struct literal of their types (core tests, iOS,
web), so the types and the literal updates must land together to compile. Moving the window query into its own patch
would leave fields that nothing reads. The iOS mapping is ~20 lines, the recognizer's per-touch device ~30.

Upstream PR: not filed yet.

